G WITH \nACTION TAKEN BY THE GOVERNMENT: \n  \nS. No. State Action taken by the Government \n1. Sikkim Site survey carried out and the site found not suitable. \nState Govt. to indicate another site."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |558", "t": "Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 558\nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY 26TH FEBRUARY.2O16\n\"SUKANYA SAMRIDDHI YOJANA''\nNo 558.\nSMT. RITI PATHAK:\nWillthe Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:-\n(a) the number of Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana ( SSY) accounts opened so far, State/UT-wise;\nand\n(b) the steps takenlbeing taken by the Government to popularise ssy?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE\n(sHRr JAYANT STNHA)\n(a): Madam. the number of accounts opened under Sukanya Samriddhi Yojarra, upto 3f\"'\nDecember. 2015, State/UT-wise are given below:-\ns |\\q\n'4\nI\nState No. of Accounts\nAndaman and Nicobar 654\ni--\n4\n6\nAndhra Pradesh\nArunachal Pradesh\n7 Chhattisgarh\nB Dadar anO f.f agaihaueli\n144796\n230373\n114799\n24475\n362084\n900249\n293420\n2923\nnsilm \n\"\nBihar\n9 Diman and Diu\n10 Delhi\n11 Goa\n12 Gujarat\n13 -Hary\"n\"\n14 Himachal Pradesh\n15 . Jammu & Kashmir\n16 Jharkhand\n17 Karnataka\n18 KtK;;ala\n19 Lakshadweep\n20 \n- \nvr\"o@Trade.h\nffrf-anar\"sntra21 l{?\n22 Manipur\n23 MeghatayaMegh\n24 Mizoram\n17\n251445\n25 Nagaland 3447\n\n26\nzl\n28\n29\n30\n31\n32\n33\n34\n35\nJO\nPuducherry\nPunjab\nRajasthan\nsiirm- -\nTamil Nadu\nTelanga_na_ ,-_\nTripura\nUttar Pradesh\nUttarakhand\n264591\n1 1 59344\n275574\n1 1615\n91 951 2\n225201\nWest Bengal 543/'49\n(b) The Government of lndia, has taken steps to popularise the scheme by carrying out publicity\nthrough print and electronic/Audio Visual media on an all-lndia basis."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |559", "t": "Recognition of Ayurveda Colleges", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(AYUSH)\nLOK  SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.559\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH FEBRUARY, 2016\nRECOGNITION OF AYURVEDA COLLEGES\n559. SHRI YOGI ADITYA NATH:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the of various Ayurveda Colleges have been derecognised in recent times due to lack\nof teachers and infrastructures in the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UTwise; and\n(c) the steps taken by the Government for promotion of Ayurveda education in the country?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) No Sir.\n(b) Does not arise.\n(c) The Central Government has taken steps for the promotion of Ayurveda education in the\ncountry by approving the regulations for Ayurveda colleges notified by the CCIM as per details\ngiven below:\ni. The Establishment of New Medical College, Opening of New or Higher Course\nof Study or Training and Increase of Admission Capacity by a Medical College\nRegulations, 2003 read with amendment Regulations of 2013.\nii. The Indian Medicine Central Council (Permission to Existing Medical Colleges)\nRegulations, 2006.\niii. Indian Medicine Central Council (Minimum Standard Requirement s of Ayurveda\nColleges and attached Hospitals) Regulations, 2012 read with amendm ent Regulation\nof 2013.\niv. Indian Medicine Central Council (Minimum Standards of Education in Indian\nMedicine (Amendment) Regulations, 2012 read with amendment Regulations of 2013.\n\nv. Indian Medicine Central Council (Post Graduate Ayurveda Education)\nRegulations, 2012.\nvi. Indian Medicine Central Council (Post -Graduate Diploma Course) Regulations,\n2010 read with amendment Regulations of 2013.\nTwo new institutes viz. All India Institute of Ayurveda, New Delhi and North Eastern\nInstitute of Ayurveda & Homoeopathy, Shillong are in advanced stage of construction.\nFurther, the assistance given by the Government of India under the component of\nDevelopment of AYUSH Institutions of NAM is facilitating improvement in infrastructure in\nAYUSH Colleges and att ached Hospitals and thereby improving quality of existing educational\nInstitutions.\n………………"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |56", "t": "Passengers in Delhi Metro", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 56 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 24, 2016 \n \nPASSENGERS IN DELHI METRO \n \nNo. 56. SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n   \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the number of passengers in Delhi Metro train has \nincreased; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the measures being taken by the Government to reduce the \nfootfall and make the journey comfortable? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a): Yes, Madam. \n(b): The details of total ridership of Delhi Metro received from Delhi \nMetro Rail Corporation Ltd. (DMRC) for the last three years are as \nunder:             \n                                                                                                  Cont… \n\n-2- \nYear Total Ridership % increase  \nover the last year \n2013 77,72,72,891 -- \n2014 86,01,53,294 10.66 % \n2015 92,25,51,128 7.25 % \n \n(c): Delhi Metro Rail Corporation Ltd. (DMRC) has informed that \ninstead of reducing footfalls, they plan to enhance capacity by \nproviding more coaches to make journey comfortable.  For this, 420 \nadditional coaches are approved for procurement; out of which 84 \ncoaches have been commissioned into service and the balance 336 \ncoaches will be inducted by December, 2017 in phases.   \nx-x-x-x-x "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |560", "t": "Anaemia among Women and Adolescent Girls", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 560\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH FEBRUARY, 2016\nANAEMIA AMONG WOMEN AND ADOLESCENT GIRLS\n560. SHRI SANJAY DHOTRE:\nSHRI KESINENI NANI:\nSHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB:\nSHRI DEVAJIBHAI G. FATEPARA:\nSHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the cases of Anaemia among women and adolescent girls and deaths therefrom have\nincreased in the country despite several measures taken by the Government;\n(b) if so, the details thereof indicating the number of such incidences reported during each of the\nlast three years and the current year, State/UT-wise including Andhra Pradesh;\n(c) the details of the schemes/ programmes being implemented by the Government to check\nanaemia cases in the country;\n(d) the funds sanctioned, released and utilized during each of the last three years and the current\nyear, programme/schemewise and State/UT-wise;\n(e) whether the cases of irregularities/ corruption in implementation of the said\nschemes/programmes in the country have come to the notice of the Government during the said\nperiod, if so, the details thereof along with the remedial ste ps taken by the Government in this\nregard; and\n(f) the other steps taken by the Government to tide over the challenge of anaemia?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (b): As per the National Family Health Survey -3 (NFHS -3)    (2005 -06), 55 percent\nwomen and 56 percent adolescent girls were anaemic. However, r ecently conducted National\nFamily Health Survey-4 (NFHS-4) (2015-16), provides information for 15 States & UTs till date,\nwhich shows a decline in anaemia prevalence in women (age 15 to 49 years) in most of the se\nStates as compared to NFHS-3.\n\nThe State-wise details of a naemia prevalence in women and adolescent g irls as per the\nNFHS-3 and NFHS-4 (only for 15 States/UTs) are provided in Annexure-1.\n(c) to (f): Government has taken many steps in order to check anaemia in the country. The\ndetails of these schemes/programmes are as below:\n The Ministry of Health & Family Welfare has launched  National Iron Plus Initiative for\nprevention and treatment of anaemia in a life cycle approach as under:\n For all under -five children, there is provision of supervised biweekly iron folic acid\nsupplementation by ASHA\n Weekly IFA tablet supplementation to children 5 – 10 years and adolescents 11 – 19\nyears\n Bi-annual Deworming to all children 1 – 19 years\n Every pregnant woman is given 100 tablets of iron and folic acid, after the first\ntrimester, to be taken 1 tablet daily and same is continued during the post natal period.\nPregnant women, who are found to be clinically anaemic, are given additional 100\ntablets for taking two tablets daily. This has been now expanded to 6 month during\nANC and 6 month during PNC.\n Universal screen ing of pregnant women for anaemia is a part of ante -natal care and all\nseverely anaemic mothers are line listed and followed up for comprehensive management at\nhigher facilities.\n Identification and tracking of severely anaemic cases at all the sub centres and PHCs for\ntheir timely management.\n Both the Health Management Information System & Mother Child Tracking S ystem are\nreporting the cases of anaemic and severely anaemic pregnant women.\n In malaria endemic areas, to tackle the problem of anaemia due to m alaria particularly in\npregnant women and children, Long Lasting Insecticide Nets (LLINs) and Insecticide\nTreated Bed Nets (ITBNs) are being distributed.\n Health and nutrition education through IEC & BCC to promote dietary diversification,\ninclusion of iron folate rich food as well as food items that promotes iron absorption.\n Safe Motherhood Booklet is being distributed to the pregnant women for educating them on\ndietary diversification and promotion of consumption of IFA.\n Funds for anaemia prevention and co ntrol programme for women is being provided as a part\nof annual programme implementation plan budget to the States/UTs which are approved in\nthe National Programme Coordination Committee.\n The details of fund sanctioned and utilized for the anaemia prevent ion and control\nprogramme for adolescent girls during the last three years are provided in Annexure 2.\n The programmes related to anaemia are being implemented by the States/UTs. However, no\ncase of corruption and irregularities in implementation of these schemes/programmes has\nbeen reported in last three years.\n……………\n\nAnnexure 1\nAnaemia Prevalence in Adolescent girls and Women as per NFHS-3 (2005-06)\nand NFHS-4 (2015-16)\nSr.\nNo. STATES\nAnaemia Prevalence\nNFHS-3 (2005-06) NFHS-4 (2015-16)\nAdolescent Girls\n(15-19 years)\nWomen of reproductive\nage group (15-49)\nWomen of reproductive\nage group (15-49)\nAll India 55.8 55.3 NA\n1 AN Islands NA NA 65.7\n2 Andhra P. 68.3 62.9 60\n3 Arunachal P. 51.2 50.6 NA\n4 Assam 67.8 69.5 NA\n5 Bihar 66.4 67.4 60."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |561", "t": "NFHS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 561\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH FEBRUARY, 2016\nNFHS\n561. SHRI DHANANJAY MAHADIK:\nDR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT:\nSHRI SATAV RAJEEV:\nSHRI ABHISHEK SINGH:\nSHRI MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO:\nSHRI SHIVKUMAR UDASI:\nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:\nSHRI R. PARTHIPAN:\nSHRI T. RADHAKRISHNAN:\nADV. JOICE GEORGE:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has conducted National Family Health Survey (NFHS), 2015-16;\n(b) if so, the details thereof State/ UT-wise along with the key finding therein;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to revive the structure and time dura tion of NFHS in order\nto make it more effective in assessing various health indicators specially maternal and child\nheath indicators; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) & (b): The fourth round of National Family Health Survey (NFHS -4), 2015-16 has been\nlaunched. The survey is carried out in two phases covering all States and Union territories.  The\nfield work in the States / UTs covered in the first phase except Assam, have been completed and\nkey results published for 13 States and 2 UTs.  A statement showing estimates of some key\nindicators for these 15 States/UTs is annexed. The field work for Assam and States/UTs\ncovered in the second phase has commenced.\n(c ) & (d): The Government has taken  a decision to dispense with various   health surveys  of\nsimilar nature with varying periodicity and to have an integrated health survey namely, “National\nFamily Health Survey” with a periodicity of three years,  to provide dist rict and above level data\non  fertility, infant and child mortality, family planning practices, maternal and child health,\nreproductive health, nutrition, anaemia, prevalence of HIV etc, covering all the States/UTs in the\ncountry to facilitate availability of data and to assess and monitor performance of health\nprogrammes / schemes at regular interval.\n\nAnnexure\nKey indicators from NFHS-4 (2015-16).\nS.No. State/UT Current use of\nFamily\nPlanning\n(Any modern\nmethod)\n(%)\nMothers who\nhad full\nantenatal care\n(%)\nInstitutional\nbirths\n(%)\nChildren aged\n12-23 months\nfully\nimmunized\n(%)\nChildren\nunder 5\nyears who\nare stunted\n(%)\nChildren\nunder 5\nyears who\nare wasted\n(%)\nChildren\naged 6-59\nmonths who\nare anaemic\n(<11.0 g/dl)\n(%)\nWomen aged\n15-49 years\nwho are\nanaemic\n(<11.0 g/dl)\n(%)\n1 Goa 24.8 63.4 96.9 88.4 20.1 21.9 48.3 31.3\n2 Haryana 59.4 19.5 80.5 62.2 34.0 21.2 71.7 62.7\n3 Karnataka 51.3 32.9 94.3 62.6 36.2 26.1 60.9 44.8\n4 Meghalaya 21.9 23.5 51.4 61.5 43.8 15.3 48.0 56.2\n5 Madhya Pradesh 49.6 11.4 80.8 53.6 42.0 25.8 68.9 52.5\n6 Sikkim 45.9 39.0 94.7 83.0 29.6 14.2 55.1 34.9\n7 Tamil Nadu 52.6 45.0 99.0 69.7 27.1 19.7 50.7 55.1\n8 Tripura 42.8 7.6 79.9 54.5 24.3 16.8 48.3 54.5\n9 Uttarakhand 49.3 11.5 68.6 57.7 33.5 19.5 59.8 45.2\n10 West Bengal 57.0 21.8 75.2 84.4 32.5 20.3 54.2 62.5\n11 Bihar 23.3 3.3 63.8 61.7 48.3 20.8 63.5 60.3\n12 Andhra Pradesh 69.4 43.9 91.6 65.3 31.4 17.2 58.6 60.0\n13 Telangana 56.9 42.2 91.5 68.1 28.1 18.0 60.7 56.7\n14 Andaman &\nNicobar Islands\n48.3 53.6 96.6 73.2 23.3 18.9 49.0 65.7\n15 Puducherry 61.2 55.6 99.9 91.3 23.7 23.6 44.9 52.4"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |562", "t": "Air Ambulance Services", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 562\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH FEBRUARY, 2016\nAIR AMBULANCE SERVICES\n562. DR. SHASHI THAROOR:\nSHRI RAYAPATI SAMBASIVA RAO:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to introduce air ambulance services in important and\npremier Government/private hospitals across the country to facilit ate transfer of organs, patients\nand medical experts among the medical institutions;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the details of Government/private hospitals already using\nthis service; and\n(c) the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to introduce such services to\nstrengthen medical mobility and knowledge sharing between two regions?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) & (b): No such pro posal is presently under consideration of Department of Health &\nFamily Welfare. Further, health is a state subject and details of Government/private hospitals\nusing such a service are not maintained centrally.\n(c ): Air ambulance services are required only in cases when the retrieved organs cannot be\nallocated within the State and are required to be transferred expeditiously to other States for\ntransplantation. Since the number of organs currently harvested and requ ired to be transported\nto other States/over long distances will be very few, the facility of air ambulance services is not\nproposed at present.\n………………"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |563", "t": "FM Services", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.563 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.02.2016 \n \nFM SERVICES \n563. SHRI HARIOM SINGH RATHORE,  \nSHRI BIRENDRA KUMAR CHOUDHARY, \n SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE,  \nSHRI RADHESHYAM BISWAS \nSHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING   \nbe pleased to state : \n(a) the number of district headquarters of the country in which the facility of FM \nservices has been provided so far, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b) whether the Government has any proposal to set up more FM Radio Stations \nincluding private FM Stations in areas which have not yet been covered under FM \nfacility so far; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise and location-wise including Bihar along \nwith the time by which the said facility is likely to be started; \n \n(d) whether the Government is aware that FM Radio Stations sanctioned for \ninternational border districts including Karimganj in Assam has not been started yet, if \nso, the reasons therefor and the time by which it is likely to be started; and \n \n(e) whether the Government is earning revenue through FM Radio, if so, the details \nthereof along with revenue earned during each of the last three years and the current \nyears? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING, \n{COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)} \n\n(a) : 328 District Headquarters are having the facility of All India Radio (AIR) FM services.       \nState/UT-wise details are given in Annexure-I.  78 District Headquarters are also having \nthe facility of private FM radio services.  State/UT-wise details are at Annexure-II. \n(b) & (c): The Cabinet, in its meeting held on 7.7.2011, approved the “Policy Guidelines \non Expansion of FM radio broadcasting services through private agencies (Phase-III)”.  \nFM Phase -III Policy extends FM radio services to 227 new cities in addition to 67 \nexisting cities of FM Phase-II having vacant channels.  Therefore, under FM Phase-III \na total of 839 new FM radio stations are to be set up in 294 cities. State -wise details \nare at Annexure-III.    Government conducted e-Auction of First batch of FM Phase -\nIII comprising 135 vacant channels in 69 existing cities of FM Phase -II. State-wise \ndetails are at Annexure-IV. Of these 91 channels in 54 cities were  issued Letters of \nIntent. State -wise details are at Annexure-V. These channels are likely to start \noperations within 12 months of issue of Letter of Intent. New AIR FM Broadcasting \nStations   are being set up at 86   locations in the country including 54 nos. of 100 W \nFM Relay transmitters.   State/UT–wise details are given in Annexure-VI.  These new \nFM stations are likely to be commissioned within the current plan period (i.e. March \n2017). \n(d) :   At  Karimganj  in  Assam,   1 kW FM setup of AIR has been installed recently. \nThe centre is likely to be commissioned during this year. \n(e) :  Details of revenue earne d by the Government from the private FM radio \nbroadcasters during the last three years and current year are at Annexure-VII and \nDetails of consolidated revenue  earned by commercials and sharing of  AIR FM \ninfrastructures with Private  FM Broadcasters  during each of the last three years and \nthe current year are given as below :  \nFinancial Year  Revenue earned by commercials and sharing \nof  AIR FM infrastructures \n(Rs. in Cr) \n2012-13 102.78 \n2013-14 130.64 \n2014-15 128.29 \n2015-16 113.99 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.563 FOR REPLY ON 26.02.2016  \nDistrict Headquarter-wise details of A.I.R.  FM Broadcasting Centres \nS.N. DISTRICT STATES \n1.  ANANTPUR ANDHRA PRADESH \n2.  CHITTOOR ANDHRA PRADESH \n3.  CUDDAPAH ANDHRA PRADESH \n4.  EAST GODAVARI ANDHRA PRADESH \n5.  GUNTUR ANDHRA PRADESH \n6.  KRISHNA ANDHRA PRADESH \n7.  KURNOOL ANDHRA PRADESH \n8.  NELLORE ANDHRA PRADESH \n9.  PRAKASAM ANDHRA PRADESH \n10.  SRIKAKULAM ANDHRA PRADESH \n11.  VISHAKHAPATNAM  ANDHRA PRADESH \n12.  CHANGLANG AR.  PRADESH \n13.  EAST SIANG AR.  PRADESH \n14.  LOHIT AR.  PRADESH \n15.  LOWER DIBANG AR.  PRADESH \n16.  LOWER SUBANSIRI AR.  PRADESH \n17.  PAPUM PARE AR.  PRADESH \n18.  TAWANG AR.  PRADESH \n19.  TIRAP  AR.  PRADESH \n20.  UPPER SIANG AR.  PRADESH \n21.  UPPER SUBANSIRI AR.  PRADESH \n22.  WEST KAMENG AR.  PRADESH \n\nS.N. DISTRICT STATES \n23.  WEST SIANG AR.  PRADESH \n24.  CACHAR ASSAM \n25.  DHUBRI ASSAM \n26.  DIBRUGARH ASSAM \n27.  DIMA HASAO  ASSAM \n28.  JORHAT ASSAM \n29.  KAMRUP METROPOLITAN  ASSAM \n30.  KOKRAJHAR ASSAM \n31.   LAKHIMPUR ASSAM \n32.  NOWGONG ASSAM \n33.  SIVASAGAR ASSAM \n34.  SONITPUR ASSAM \n35.  TINSUKIA ASSAM \n36.  ARARIA BIHAR \n37.  AURANGABAD BIHAR \n38.  BETIAH BIHAR \n39.  BHAGALPUR BIHAR \n40.  GAYA BIHAR \n41.  KISHANGANJ BIHAR \n42.  MADUBANI BIHAR \n43.  MOTIHARI BIHAR \n44.  MUZAFFARPUR BIHAR \n45.  PATNA   BIHAR \n46.  PURNEA  BIHAR \n47.  ROHTAS BIHAR \n48.  SITAMARHI BIHAR \n49.  SUPAUL BIHAR \n50.  BASTAR  CHATTISGAR"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |564", "t": "LIC Norms", "head": "F. No.H-12016/01/2016-Ins.I\nGovernment of India\nMinistry of Finance\nDepartment of Financial Services\nInsurance-1 Section\n*****\nSubject: - Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. †564 admitted for 26th \nFebruary 2016 raised by Shrimati Neelam Sonker regarding \nLIC Norms.\n Shrimati Neelam Sonker, Hon’ble Member of Parliament (LS) has raised a \nQuestion regarding “LIC Norms”.  The text of the Question is reproduced below:-\n(a) whether the Government has fixed any new norms to make life insurance products \naccessible and customer friendly; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any time schedule has been fixed for compliance of these new norms; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof?\n2. Based on the information received from LIC and IRDA, a draft reply to the \nQuestion has been attempted and placed below for consideration/approval please.\n(S K Mohanty)\nUS Ins.I\nDir(Ins.I)\n\nGovernment of India\nMinistry of Finance\nDepartment of Financial Services\nLok Sabha\nUnstarred Question No. †564\nTo be answered on Friday, February 26, 2016/Phalguna 7, 1937 (Saka)\nLIC Norms\nQuestion\n†564. Shrimati Neelam Sonker:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE\nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has fixed any \nnew norms to make life insurance \nproducts accessible and customer \nfriendly; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any time schedule has been fixed \nfor compliance of these new norms; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nAnswer\nTo be answered by\nMinister of State in the Ministry of Finance\n(Shri Jayant Sinha)\n \n(a) to (d): Insurance Regulatory and Development \nAuthority of India (IRDAI) has issued the following new \nregulations for streamlining and simplifying the design \nand procedure related to life insurance products:\n1. IRDA (Linked Insurance Product) Regulations, \n2013 dated 16th February 2013.  All existing \nindividual linked life insurance products were to \nbecome compliant or withdrawn by 1st October 2013 \nwhich was later extended up to 1st January 2014.\n2. IRDA (Non-Linked Insurance Product) Regulations \n2013 dated 16th February 2013.  All existing \nindividual Non-linked life insurance products were \nto become compliant or withdrawn by 1st October \n2013 which was later extended up to 1st January \n2014.\n3. IRDAI (Health Insurance) Regulations, 2013 dated \n16th February 2013. All existing individual health \ninsurance products were to become compliant or \nwithdrawn by 1st October 2013.\n4. IRDAI (Micro Insurance Regulations) 2015 dated \n13th March 2015.  All Micro Insurance Products \nwere to become compliant with new norms by 31st \nDecember 2015 which has now been extended upto \n31st March, 2016.\n\nGovernment of India\nMinistry of Finance\nDepartment of Financial Services\nLok Sabha\nUnstarred Question No. †564\nTo be answered on Friday, February 26, 2016/Phalguna 7, 1937 (Saka)\nLIC Norms\n†564. Shrimati Neelam Sonker:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE\nbe pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has fixed any new norms to make life insurance products \naccessible and customer friendly; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any time schedule has been fixed for compliance of these new norms; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nAnswer\nMinister of State in the Ministry of Finance\n(Shri Jayant Sinha)\n(a) to (d): Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) has issued the \nfollowing new regulations for streamlining and simplifying the design and procedure related \nto life insurance products:\n1. IRDA (Linked Insurance Product) Regulations, 2013 dated 16th February 2013.  All \nexisting individual linked life insurance products were to become compliant or withdrawn \nby 1st October 2013 which was later extended up to 1st January 2014.\n2. IRDA (Non-Linked Insurance Product) Regulations 2013 dated 16th February 2013.  All \nexisting individual Non-linked life insurance products were to become compliant or \nwithdrawn by 1st October 2013 which was later extended up to 1st January 2014.\n3. IRDAI (Health Insurance) Regulations, 2013 dated 16th February 2013. All existing \nindividual health insurance products were to become compliant or withdrawn by 1st \nOctober 2013.\n4. IRDAI (Micro Insurance Regulations) 2015 dated 13th March 2015.  All Micro Insurance \nProducts were to become compliant with new norms by 31st December 2015 which has \nnow been extended upto 31st March, 2016.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |565", "t": "Sharing of Sensitive Information", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE \nLOK  SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.565 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH FEBRUARY, 2016 \n \n \nSHARING  OF  SENSITIVE  INFORMATION \n \n565. DR. SUNIL  BALIRAM  GAIKWAD: \n SHRI  ASHOK  SHANKARRAO  CHAVAN: \n SHRI  GAJANAN  KIRTIKAR: \n SHRI  SUDHEER  GUPTA: \n SHRI  BIDYUT  BARAN  MAHATO: \n KUNWAR  HARIBANSH  SINGH:    \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE    j{kk ea=h     \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether some defence personnel were arrested for sharing sensitive details on \nsocial media and if so, the details thereof incl uding any action taken against such \npersonnel; \n \n(b)  whether the Government has issued any advisory of digital do ’s and don ’ts to \ndefence personnel and their family members; \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d)  the other steps taken by the Governmen t to check the misuse of sensitive details \npertaining to defence services? \n                  \n   A    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                            (SHRI MANOHAR PARRIKAR) \n     j{kk ea=h                 ¼Jh euksgj ijhZdj½ \n \n(a) During last two years, two personnel each from Army & Air Force \nwere arrested for sharing sensitive information on social media.  Air Force \npersonnel have also been dismissed from service. \n(b) to (d): Comprehensive advisories on digital do ’s and don’ts have been \nissued to defence personnel and their family members. \nArmed Forces have exclusive secure networks which are air -gapped \nand physically segregated from public networks.  Awareness drive on \nthreats from social media and internet are being u ndertaken to sensitize \ndefence personnel.  Cyber security drills are also carried out to validate the \ncapabilities of the designated cyber agencies. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |566", "t": "Permanent Buildings for Anganwadi Centres", "head": "Anne