28 West Bengal 164.135 184.3735 108.847 100.934 404.916 348.516 596.8825 376.50781 \n29 Puducherry  0 0 12.00 00 0 0 0 0 \n  TOTAL 7489.4855 6677.999 6588.99857 5973. 77355 16287.2536 17228.81133 17501.9959 11885.65782 \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |758", "t": "Fertility of Soil", "head": "     \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.758  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 1ST MARCH, 2016 \n \nFERTILITY OF SOIL \n \n758.    SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has evaluated the fertility of soil/arable land across the country, \nif so, the norms adopted for the purpose and the extent of micronutrients deficiency noticed in \nthe country, State-wise; \n \n(b)  the details of the schemes and projects un der implementation to check the declining \nfertility of agricultural land and improve the fertility of soil for increasing agricultural production in \nthe country including Gujarat; and \n \n(c)  the success achieved thereunder, so far, in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्राऱय में राज्य मंत्री     (SHRI  MOHANBHAI  KUNDARIYA) \n \n(a):     Government has launched Soil Health Card Scheme to evaluate fertility of soils/ arable \nland across the country and to provide soil test based fertilizer recommendati on to farmers in \nthe country. The  basic norms adopted for the purpose are :- \n \n(i)    The soil samples collection should be GIS based and \n(ii)   In irrigated areas, samples will be drawn in a grid of 2.5 ha and in rainfed areas sampling \nwill be done in 10 ha. Grid. \n        \nThe assessment made by Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) under All India \nCoordinated Research Project on ‘Micro and Secondary Nutrients and Pollutant Elements in \nsoils and Plants’  revealed that nearly 43.0, 12.1, 5.5, 5.4, an d 18.3 per cent samples were \ndeficient in Zinc, Iron, Manganes e, Coper and Boron , respectively across the country.  State -\nwise details are given in Annexure.  \n   \n(b):    Government is implementing Soil Health Management and Soil Health Card Schemes \nduring 12th Plan period in the country including Gujarat.  S oil test based application of fertilizers \nis essential to improve soil health and productivity. The Government is promoting soil test based \nbalanced and judicious use of chemical fertilizers, bio -fertilizers and locally available organic \nmanures like Farm Yard Manure, compost, Vermi Compost and Green manure to maintain soil \nhealth and its productivity.   ‘Soil Health Card’ (SHC) scheme has been launched in February \n2015 to assist State Governments to evaluate fertility in all 14 crore farm holdings and issue soil  \nContd…2/- \n\n \n \n-2- \n \n \nhealth cards to farmers regularly in a cycle of 2 years.    Soil health cards provide information to \nfarmers on nutrient status of their soil along with recommendations on appro priate dosage of \nnutrients to be applied for improving soil health and its fertility. \n \n(c)      In 2015-16, so far, under Soil Health Card Scheme aginst target of 100 lakh soil samples, \n81 lakh collected and against the target of 5 crore Soil health Cards 1.12 crore cards issued to \nfarmers & 2 crore cards under printing. \n \n          Under Soil Health Management  (SHM) scheme, setting up of 103 new static and 77 new \nmobile soil testing laboratories and  269 existing Soil Testing Laboratories strengthened have  \nbeen sanctioned during 2014-15  and 2015-16. \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure   \n \nExtent of micronutrient deficiency in soils of different states of India \nState \n  \nDTPA-extractable micronutrients Hot water soluble B \nNo. of \nsamples \nPercent samples deficient \nNo. of \nsamples \nPercent \nsamples \ndeficient Zn Fe Cu Mn \nAndhra Pradesh 6723 22.3 16.8 1.0 1.7 3216 2.8 \nAssam 5216 25.5 0.0 3.8 0.0 5216 11.9 \nBihar 7304 41.4 12.3 1.8 7.8 3597 33.3 \nGujarat 5470 23.1 23.9 0.4 6.3 5470 17.9 \nHaryana 5673 15.3 21.6 5.2 6.1 5673 3.3 \nHimachal Pradesh 642 1.4 7.8 0.2 22.1 161 8.7 \nJharkhand 443 20.3 0.0 0.5 0.0 443 56.0 \nMadhya Pradesh 7580 61.7 9.6 0.2 1.6 3330 2.4 \nMaharashtra 8278 54.0 21.5 0.2 3.8 489 54.8 \nOdisha 2349 22.7 1.8 0.3 1.1 2349 52.5 \nPunjab 2181 16.6 6.2 3.6 15.2 1083 17.5 \nTamil Nadu 31080 65.5 10.6 13.0 7.9 31080 19.9 \nTelangana 4799 26.9 17.0 1.4 3.8 2776 16.1 \nUttar Pradesh 4788 33.1 7.6 6.3 6.5 4323 16.2 \nUttarakhand 2575 9.6 1.4 1.4 4.7 2575 7.0 \nWest Bengal 2363 11.9 0.0 1.2 0.9 1849 46.9 \nAll India 97464 43.0 12.1 5.4 5.5 73630 18.3 \n \n \n********  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |759", "t": "Schemes Related to B.R. Ambedkar", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †759\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.03.2016\n†759. SCHEMES RELATED TO B.R. AMBEDKAR \nSHRI ARJUN MEGHWAL: \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any plan for some innovative work in the \nmemory of Dr. B.R. Ambedkar; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has formulated any action plan for expansion of the places \nlinked to the life of Baba Saheb Ambedkar; and \n(d) if so, the number of places identified so far to start expansion?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI VIJAY SAMPLA)\n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. The details of the activities planned for the yearlong Celebrations of \n125th Birth Anniversary of Dr. B. R. Ambedkar in his memory are at Annexure.\n(c) & (d): As a part of birth anniversary celebrations, the government has taken up \nconstruction of Dr. Ambedkar International Centre at 15 Janpath, New Delhi, Dr. Ambedkar \nNational Memorial at 26 Alipur Road, Delhi and renovation of Deeksha Bhumi, Nagpur, \nMaharshtra.\n*****\n\nANNEXURE\n Annexure in respect of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.759 part (a) &(b) to be \nanswered on 01.03.2016 regarding ‘Schemes Related to B. R. Ambedkar’ raised by \nShri Arjun Meghwal\n(i) Declaration and Celebration of the 14th April as ‘Rashtriya Samrasata \nDivas’\n \n(ii) Declaration and Celebration of 26th November as ‘Constitution Day’\n(iii) Construction of Dr.Ambedkar International Centre, 15 Janpath, New Delhi \n(iv) Release of Commemorative Postage Stamp.\n(v) Release of Commemorative coins of Rs.10 & Rs.125.\n(vi) Organise National Conference of Dalit Entrepreneurs on ideas & thoughts \nof Dr.Ambedkar on entrepreneurship and Industrialisation.\n(vii) All India Online Quiz Competition on the Constitution of India and life & \nwork of Dr. B.R. Amebdkar.\n(viii) Display a Tableau on Dr.Ambedkar in Republic Day Parade.\n(ix) Setting up of Virtual Museum on life and works of Dr.Ambedkar.\n(x) Releasing audio CDs of the Collected Works of Dr.Ambedkar.\n(xi) Organizing lectures through Dr.Ambedkar Chairs.\n(xii) Organizing Dr.Ambedkar essay competition.\n(xiii) Sponsoring of 100 students/research scholars to visit London School of \nEconomics, UK and Columbia University, USA where Dr.Ambedkar \nstudied.\n(xiv) Converting 17 volumes of Collected Works of Baba Saheb Ambedkar  into \nBraille\n(xv) Organizing ‘Mock Parliament Sessions’ on the constituent assembly debates \nin schools\n(xvi) Organizing the ‘Run for National Unity’.\n(xvii) Construction of Dr.Ambedkar National Memorial at 26 Alipur Road Delhi"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |76", "t": "Exchange of Nuclear Installations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.76 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.02.2016 \n \nEXCHANGE OF NUCLEAR INSTALLATIONS \n \n76. DR. C. GOPALAKRISHNAN: \n SHRIMATI V. SATHYA BAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether India and Pakistan have recently exchanged the list \nof their nuclear installations and facilities under the \nAgreement on the Prohibition of Attack against Nuclear \ninstallations; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the purpose behind exchanging the said list? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) to (c)  For the twenty fifth consecutive year, India and \nPakistan, on 1 January 2016, through diplomatic channels, \nsimultaneously at New Delhi and Islamabad, exchanged lists of \nnuclear installations and facilities covered under the Agreement on \nthe Prohibition of Attack against Nuclear installations and facilities \nbetween India and Pakistan. \n \nThe Agreement was signed on 31 December 1988 and  entered \ninto force on 27 January 1991. Under the Agreement, the two \ncountries are to inform each other , on January 1 of every calendar \nyear, of the nuclear installations and facilities to be covered under \nthe Agreement. The Agreement is an importan t confidence building \nmeasure between India and Pakistan  aimed at avoiding attack \nagainst each other’s nuclear installations included in the lists \nexchanged. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|7|UNSTARRED |760", "t": "Welfare of Fishermen", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING AND FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 760 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01ST MARCH, 2016 \n \nWELFARE OF FISHERMEN \n \n760.  KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृ षि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \n be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has comprehensive data on the demographics and number of \nfishermen in the country, if so, the details thereof, State-wise; \n \n(b) the details of the number of Model Fishermen Villages, number of houses, worksheds and \ntubewells in villages developed in Karnataka from 2011 till date, year-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has a proposal to increase the insurance cover period for active \nfishermen from the existing 12 months; and \n \n(d) if so, the details of fishermen benefited from the scheme along with the amount received by \nthem from 2011 till date, year-wise and State –wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n   (a): A statement showing details of the number of fishermen  in the country S tate-wise is \nfurnished at Annexure - I. \n \n   (b):  No work sheds and tube wells have been sanctioned by the Department of Animal \nHusbandry, Dairying and Fisheries in the State of Karnataka during 2011 -12 to 2015 -16. \nFurther, the M odel fishermen villages concept has not been adopted by the State \nGovernment of Karnataka.  However, the Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying and \nFisheries under the Central Sector Sche me “National Scheme of Welfare of Fishermen” and \nthe State Govt. of Karnataka under the State Sector have sanctioned  a total of 7,865 houses \nfor fishermen during the said period.  The year wise details of these sanctioned houses for \nfishermen are as below: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \nYear 2011-12 2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 Total \nNo. of houses \nsanctioned for \nfishermen \n1600 1400 1000 2865 1000 7865 \n \n(c): No, Madam. \n \n(d): State -wise details of fishermen benefited, and central funds released by the Ministry of \nAgriculture and Farmers Welfare , Government of India under the Central Sector Scheme on \n“National Scheme of Welfare of Fishermen ” from 2011-12 to 2015-16 are furnished respectively \nat Annexure- II & Annexure- III. \n \n******* \n\nANNEXURE- I \n \nStatement referred to in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 760 for \nreply on 01st March, 2016 regarding “Welfare of fishermen” in Karnataka put in by \nKUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE \n 